{"from": "artifact:cyrene-ossuary-alexander", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-21-alexander-rufus", "grade": "D", "note": "A Kidron Valley cave of Cyrenian Jews (excavated 1941 by Sukenik) held an ossuary inscribed twice in Greek 'Alexander son of Simon'; the fetched source states it cannot be certain this refers to the same family (Mark names Simon's son Alexander) - and the find is unprovenanced, so authenticity cannot be secured."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-7-29-purple-cloak", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-17-purple-robe", "grade": "C", "note": "Simon bar Gioras in 70 CE 'buttoned upon him a purple cloak' over a white frock - the purple cloak as a garment of royal pretension in first-century Judaea, in the same period and region. It shows the custom of purple as royal garb, not the soldiers' act itself."}
{"from": "text:talmud-sanhedrin-43a", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-23-wine-myrrh", "grade": "C", "note": "The court gives one led out to be killed a grain of frankincense in a cup of wine to confuse his mind - the custom of a stupefying drink for the condemned; it names frankincense, not myrrh, and the Talmud was redacted c. 200 CE and later (Gemara c. 500 CE), so it is later memory."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-5-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-25-third-hour", "grade": "C", "note": "Mishnah Pesachim 5:1 reckons Temple rite by 'hours of the day' counted from sunrise ('eight and a half hours of the day, which is two and a half hours after midday') - the same Jewish hour-reckoning that makes 'the third hour' about nine in the morning; redacted c. 200 CE, so later than the 30s."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-28-counted-transgressors", "grade": "B", "note": "The Great Isaiah Scroll (1QIsa-a, ca. 125 BCE) contains the entire Book of Isaiah, including chapter 53 and verse 12 ('counted with the transgressors') - the text Mark quotes existed in a pre-Christian Hebrew manuscript, confirming the quotation's source text in the period."}
{"from": "text:didache-8-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-42-preparation-day", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception-era usage: the Didache (first/early second century) directs fasting 'on the fourth day and the Preparation' - the Roberts-Donaldson translation glosses 'the Preparation (Friday)', showing paraskeue was the standing name for the day before the Sabbath. An early Christian usage datum, independent of the Gospel narratives; still reception, so D."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-43-joseph-council-member", "grade": "C", "note": "Ananus the high priest 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges' in Jerusalem in 62 CE - the Jerusalem council that a bouleutes belongs to existed in this period and could convene capital cases. It attests the institution and office, not Joseph's membership."}
{"from": "text:digest-48-24-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-15-44-45-body-release", "grade": "B", "note": "Ulpian: bodies of the executed 'are not buried otherwise than if it has been requested and permitted, and sometimes it is not permitted, especially those condemned for treason' - the request-and-permission practice Mark's account of Pilate verifying the death and granting the body presupposes; same empire-wide practice in the same period. (Paulus, Digest 48.24.3, adds: bodies are 'to be given for burial to any who ask'.)"}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-07-barabbas-insurgents", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-16-26-barabbas-released", "note": "Mark adds that Barabbas was bound with fellow insurgents 'who in the insurrection had committed murder'; Matthew 27:16 calls Barabbas a notable prisoner without the insurrection detail."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-17-purple-robe", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-28-30-soldiers-mockery", "note": "Mark says the soldiers clothed him 'with purple' (15:17); Matthew 27:28 says 'a scarlet robe'. Same mockery scene, different color word."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-21-alexander-rufus", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-32-simon-compelled", "note": "Mark alone names Simon of Cyrene as 'the father of Alexander and Rufus'; Matthew 27:32 names Simon without the sons."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-21-alexander-rufus", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:romans-16-13", "note": "Romans 16:13 greets 'Rufus, the chosen in the Lord, and his mother' - traditionally connected to Mark's Rufus, but the letter does not say so; biblical cross-reference only."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-23-wine-myrrh", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-34-wine-with-gall", "note": "Mark 15:23 offers 'wine mixed with myrrh'; Matthew 27:34 'sour wine mixed with gall'. Same offer, different ingredient."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-28-counted-transgressors", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:isaiah-53-12", "note": "Isaiah 53:12 (WEB): 'he poured out his soul to death, and was counted with the transgressors' - the text Mark quotes. Cross-reference only; it does not attest."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-34-cry-forsaken", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:psalm-22-1", "note": "Psalm 22:1 (WEB): 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' - the text behind Mark's Aramaic 'Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani'. Cross-reference only; it does not attest."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-40-41-women-named", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-55-61-women-watching", "note": "Mark 15:40 names 'Salome' where Matthew 27:56 names 'the mother of the sons of Zebedee'; Mark also has 'James the less'. Same group of watching women, different naming."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-43-joseph-council-member", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-57-58-joseph-burial", "note": "Mark 15:43 calls Joseph 'a prominent council member (bouleutes) who was looking for God's Kingdom'; Matthew 27:57 calls him 'a rich man' and 'Jesus' disciple'. Same man, same act, different characterization."}
{"from": "claim:mk-15-44-45-body-release", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-27-57-58-joseph-burial", "note": "Mark 15:44-45 alone gives Pilate's astonishment at the early death, the centurion inquiry and the formal grant of the body; Matthew 27:58 has only the request and command."}
